@*
    The ways to add people to this node, presented together (2026-08-21).

    MODEL: true to offer bulk import, false to leave it out.

    FALSE ON PURPOSE for EducatorIndex and AdminIndex. Owner-confirmed 2026-08-21: educator and
    admin accounts carry more control than a learner account, so they are created ONE AT A TIME,
    deliberately, rather than pasted in from a spreadsheet. Those two pages had the bulk button
    commented out in their own markup with no explanation, which is exactly how a deliberate
    decision gets "tidied" back on by the next person. It is written down now, here and there.
*@
@model bool
@using Febris.SharedServices;
@*

    Invitations used to be a separate navbar entry, which put the newest way of adding a user
    somewhere you would never look while adding a user. It belongs here, next to the other two, so
    the decision reads as one choice with three answers rather than two buttons and a menu item.

    This partial also replaces four copies of the same markup across Index, StudentIndex,
    EducatorIndex and AdminIndex. Those copies had already drifted: two of them had lost the tooltip
    on the bulk button, so the only thing distinguishing "one plus" from "two pluses" was the number
    of identical green icons. The buttons are labelled now.

    Gating: all three targets are [Authorize(Roles = EducatorAndOrgAdmins)] at the controller, and
    every page that renders this partial already sits behind the same gate, so there is nothing to
    branch on here. WHICH roles an operator may grant is a separate question that RoleRankPolicy
    answers inside each flow.
*@
<div class="row" style="margin-bottom: 15px;">
    <div class="col-md-12">
        <div class="btn-group" role="group" aria-label="Add users">
            @* asp-route-accountType carries the role the surrounding list implies. It renders as
               nothing when ViewBag.AddUserRole is unset, which is the Comprehensive list. *@
            <a asp-controller="User" asp-action="Create" asp-route-accountType="@ViewBag.AddUserRole" class="btn btn-success"
               title="Create one account now. They are emailed a link to set their own password.">
                <i class="fa fa-user-plus"></i> Add a user
            </a>
            @if (Model)
            {
                <a asp-controller="User" asp-action="BulkCreate" class="btn btn-success"
                   title="Import many accounts at once from a spreadsheet.">
                    <i class="fa fa-users"></i> Import a list
                </a>
            }
            <a asp-controller="Invitations" asp-action="Index" class="btn btn-outline-success"
               title="Email someone a one-time link. The account is created when they accept.">
                <i class="fa fa-envelope-o"></i> Send an invitation
            </a>
            @if (User.IsLocalAdmin())
            {
                @* Guardian links joined this group in ROADMAP 17. The whole ParentLink surface
                   (controller, views, BLL, tests) shipped with no entry point anywhere in the
                   Portal -- docs/BUGS.md recorded it as invisible-unless-you-know-the-URL and the
                   System-nav fix that followed skipped it. Same reasoning as Invitations above:
                   it belongs next to the other people-management actions, not on the navbar.
                   Gated to admins here because ParentLinkController is [Authorize(OrgAdmins)],
                   which is narrower than the EducatorAndOrgAdmins gate on the hosting pages. *@
                <a asp-controller="ParentLink" asp-action="Index" class="btn btn-outline-success"
                   title="Link a parent or guardian account to a learner. Admins only.">
                    <i class="fa fa-link"></i> Guardian links
                </a>
            }
        </div>
        <div>
            <small class="text-muted">
                @if (Model)
                {
                    <text><strong>Add a user</strong> and <strong>Import a list</strong> create the account
                    straight away and email a password-setup link.</text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text><strong>Add a user</strong> creates the account straight away and emails a
                    password-setup link.</text>
                }
                <strong>Send an invitation</strong> creates nothing until the person accepts, so an
                invitation nobody takes up leaves no account behind.
            </small>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
